  {
    "id": 1194,
    "date": "2021-11-08T20:21:20+00:00",
    "from": "tour2020",
    "text": "Downtown Brawley \n\nA small city of farmers, ranchers, laborers, service workers, and support professionals. \n\nNice weather for just about 6 months out of the year, too hot for the remainder.\n\nPhoto above taken within the diamond shape below.  Post Office,  City Hall offices, Library, and city park all located within.\n\nhttps://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brawley,_California\n.",
    "media": null
  },
  {
    "id": 1195,
    "date": "2021-11-08T20:23:48+00:00",
    "from": "tour2020",
    "text": "32.97866° N, 115.53493° W",
    "media": "1195.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": 1196,
    "date": "2021-11-10T21:37:04+00:00",
    "from": "tour2020",
    "text": "Adopt a tree. A system of overnight camping develops under the tree. \n\nNotice some new developments below. \n\nThree discarded plastic soap jugs easily found at a laundromat. \n\nInside the laundromat there are wash basins that may sometimes give hot water.\n\nWithin the apparently empty soap jug we find plenty of soap to mix with the water.  The jugs have large enough lids where one can stuff single light pieces of clothing and cleaning rags.\n\nOne large jug can be cut and used as a bucket with a handle. \n\nFind an empty plastic water bottle and poke a hole into the center of the cap.  Fill the bottle with clean water for rinsing kitchen ware. Keep plenty of recycled plastic water bottles around filled with filtered water for consumption. \n\nPhoto below shows a setup that has evolved under one tree, a Velvet Mesquite tree of the Sonoran Desert.\n\nI leave the plastic bucket and water bottles behind and pack few possessions moving from tree to tree around the valley. \n\nThis system works, the weather is mild, there are hot springs around, the people may not be the brightness but they are kind.\n\nIt's a very unique place, situation, culture, a different kind of \"heartland of America\", with a very strong relationship with Mexico. \n\nIt's not a place I would stay forever,  but as a winter escape from late October to late Spring, the warm and sunshine seems to always be there.",
    "media": null
  },
  {
    "id": 1197,
    "date": "2021-11-10T21:49:34+00:00",
    "from": "tour2020",
    "text": "",
